1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,880
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:08,835
This is chapter forty-four, the final chapter: testing concurrent code.

3
00:00:09,285 --> 00:00:18,712
Concurrency bugs are the hardest to test for, because a race needs two threads inside a window a few nanoseconds wide at the same moment.

4
00:00:18,712 --> 00:00:21,326
A test that passes proves very little.

5
00:00:21,326 --> 00:00:28,207
So the goal is to make that coincidence as likely as possible, and to need it as rarely as possible.

6
00:00:29,107 --> 00:00:31,318
Three tests that look reasonable.

7
00:00:31,318 --> 00:00:35,203
The first sleeps half a second and hopes the work is done.

8
00:00:35,203 --> 00:00:41,566
It's slow on every run, and flaky whenever CI is loaded enough that half a second isn't enough.

9
00:00:42,016 --> 00:00:48,720
The second starts two threads that each increment a counter a thousand times, and checks for two thousand.

10
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:58,016
It passes against a counter with no synchronisation at all, most of the time, because the first thread often finishes before the second one starts.

11
00:00:58,466 --> 00:01:02,801
And the third mocks the inventory and asserts reserve returns true.

12
00:01:02,801 --> 00:01:05,065
It tests nothing about concurrency.

13
00:01:05,065 --> 00:01:08,365
The mock is the thing that would have had the race.

14
00:01:09,265 --> 00:01:10,686
Think in four layers.

15
00:01:10,686 --> 00:01:13,732
First, design: shrink the concurrent surface.

16
00:01:13,732 --> 00:01:16,305
It finds nothing, because it prevents.

17
00:01:16,755 --> 00:01:21,454
Second, deterministic tests of the logic, with no threads at all.

18
00:01:21,454 --> 00:01:23,261
Cheap, fast and reliable.

19
00:01:23,711 --> 00:01:30,559
Third, stress tests of the genuinely concurrent part: many threads, released together, checking invariants.

20
00:01:31,009 --> 00:01:36,321
And fourth, specialised tools like jcstress for memory-model behaviour.

21
00:01:36,771 --> 00:01:39,770
Most of the value is in the first two layers.

22
00:01:39,770 --> 00:01:50,830
If concurrency is confined to a few small components and the business logic is testable synchronously, you need stress tests for a handful of classes, not everywhere.

23
00:01:51,730 --> 00:01:54,206
Here's the design layer in practice.

24
00:01:54,206 --> 00:01:59,226
ReservationPolicy holds the decision: invalid, insufficient, or reserved.

25
00:01:59,226 --> 00:02:05,347
It's pure, so it gets ordinary unit tests for every boundary and branch, with no threads.

26
00:02:05,347 --> 00:02:13,394
InventoryItem is small, and asks only one concurrent question: does synchronized keep the available count consistent?

27
00:02:13,394 --> 00:02:16,626
That's the only class that needs a stress test.

28
00:02:17,526 --> 00:02:28,503
If a class takes its Executor as a constructor parameter, a test can pass Runnable colon colon run, an executor that runs each task immediately on the calling thread.

29
00:02:28,503 --> 00:02:35,777
Dispatch three orders, and verify three sends, with no sleeping and no polling, because it's already happened.

30
00:02:35,777 --> 00:02:38,158
That tests the logic of dispatching.

31
00:02:38,158 --> 00:02:42,059
Whether the real pool is concurrent is a separate question.

32
00:02:42,509 --> 00:02:45,712
The same idea applies to time and scheduling.

33
00:02:45,712 --> 00:02:52,685
Inject the Executor, the Clock and the scheduler, and tests can substitute deterministic versions.

34
00:02:53,585 --> 00:02:57,828
When something genuinely is asynchronous, poll with Awaitility.

35
00:02:57,828 --> 00:03:03,889
Wait at most five seconds, checking every twenty milliseconds, until the assertion passes.

36
00:03:04,339 --> 00:03:13,591
A sleep encodes a guess about how long the work takes, and it's wrong both ways: too long on a fast machine, too short on a loaded one.

37
00:03:13,591 --> 00:03:16,812
Polling with a deadline is right in both cases.

38
00:03:16,812 --> 00:03:22,089
And set the deadline generously; it only costs time when the test is failing.

39
00:03:22,989 --> 00:03:25,539
Now a stress test that really stresses.

40
00:03:25,539 --> 00:03:32,796
A thousand units of stock, thirty-two threads, a hundred attempts each, so three thousand two hundred attempts.

41
00:03:32,796 --> 00:03:39,138
Every thread waits on a latch, the start gate, and then they're all released at the same instant.

42
00:03:39,588 --> 00:03:41,435
Four details make this work.

43
00:03:41,435 --> 00:03:43,745
The start gate forces real overlap.

44
00:03:43,745 --> 00:03:47,176
More threads than cores keeps the race windows busy.

45
00:03:47,176 --> 00:03:54,434
It asserts invariants, exactly a thousand reservations and nothing left, which must hold for any interleaving.

46
00:03:54,434 --> 00:04:00,570
And it repeats twenty times with a timeout, so a deadlock fails instead of hanging the build.

47
00:04:00,570 --> 00:04:04,463
Remove synchronized, and it fails within a few repetitions.

48
00:04:05,363 --> 00:04:12,175
For code that takes several locks, check ThreadMXBean's findDeadlockedThreads during the stress run.

49
00:04:12,175 --> 00:04:17,352
It turns a hang into a precise failure that names the threads and the locks.

50
00:04:17,802 --> 00:04:22,341
And for memory-model bugs, use jcstress, OpenJDK's own harness.

51
00:04:22,341 --> 00:04:28,898
It runs two actors hundreds of millions of times across fresh JVMs and counts each outcome.

52
00:04:28,898 --> 00:04:36,895
Here, a non-volatile ready flag was seen as true while the value was still zero fourteen thousand times on ARM.

53
00:04:36,895 --> 00:04:40,642
An ordinary test would essentially never catch that.

54
00:04:40,642 --> 00:04:44,533
Use it for low-level primitives, not application code.

55
00:04:45,433 --> 00:04:47,224
Two reasons races hide.

56
00:04:47,224 --> 00:04:56,257
The JIT changes them: hoisting a read out of a loop, or reordering writes, so some bugs exist only in compiled code.

57
00:04:56,257 --> 00:04:59,761
That's why stress tests need many iterations.

58
00:04:59,761 --> 00:05:01,397
And hardware matters.

59
00:05:01,397 --> 00:05:10,819
A two-core x86 CI container is about the least likely place to expose a race, and ARM exposes reorderings that x86 hides.

60
00:05:11,269 --> 00:05:18,364
Which means a concurrent test that fails intermittently is usually correctly detecting a real race.

61
00:05:18,364 --> 00:05:23,380
Retrying it until it passes converts a detected bug into a hidden one.

62
00:05:23,380 --> 00:05:29,829
Quarantine it, raise the threads and iterations until it fails reliably, and fix the code.

63
00:05:30,729 --> 00:05:31,967
Production rules.

64
00:05:31,967 --> 00:05:34,152
Shrink the concurrent surface.

65
00:05:34,152 --> 00:05:37,065
Inject executors, clocks and schedulers.

66
00:05:37,065 --> 00:05:40,051
Use a same-thread executor to test logic.

67
00:05:40,051 --> 00:05:42,964
Use Awaitility or latches with timeouts.

68
00:05:42,964 --> 00:05:45,877
Use a start gate, and assert invariants.

69
00:05:45,877 --> 00:05:49,737
And repeat stress tests, with a timeout on every one.

70
00:05:50,187 --> 00:05:52,978
Never sleep to wait for asynchronous work.

71
00:05:52,978 --> 00:05:56,166
Never start stress threads without a start gate.

72
00:05:56,166 --> 00:06:00,152
Never mock the component whose thread safety you're testing.

73
00:06:00,152 --> 00:06:03,607
Never retry a flaky concurrent test until it passes.

74
00:06:03,607 --> 00:06:07,659
And never conclude code is thread-safe because a test passed.

75
00:06:08,559 --> 00:06:09,216
To recap.

76
00:06:09,216 --> 00:06:12,789
Less concurrent code beats more concurrent tests.

77
00:06:12,789 --> 00:06:16,362
A same-thread executor makes logic deterministic.

78
00:06:16,362 --> 00:06:18,842
Poll with a deadline, never sleep.

79
00:06:18,842 --> 00:06:23,071
Stress tests need a start gate, invariants and repetition.

80
00:06:23,071 --> 00:06:26,863
And an intermittent failure is probably a real race.

81
00:06:27,313 --> 00:06:33,681
That completes the handbook's video series, from the JDK landscape to testing concurrent code.

82
00:06:33,681 --> 00:06:35,036
Thanks for watching.
